  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Unseasonably warm through Thursday with localized smoke from\n   prescribed burns.\n\n-  Cold front Friday afternoon brings strong west winds, choppy\n   lakes, and elevated fire weather concerns.\n\n-  Cutoff low this weekend brings widespread rain to Northwest\n   Montana and a couple inches of snow to high peaks of Glacier \n   National Park.\n\nSouthwesterly flow aloft will maintain unseasonably warm and dry \nconditions across the Northern Rockies through Thursday. Isolated,\nhigh-based showers may develop across southwest Montana this \nafternoon, but measurable precipitation and operational impacts \nare unlikely. Increased prescribed burning will lead to the\npossibility of localized smoke through friday morning.\n\nA significant change begins Friday as a cold front approaches \nfrom the west. The front will cross the US-93 corridor near 6 PM \nMDT, bringing strong westerly winds. Wind gusts up toward 45 mph \nare expected across southwest Montana, persisting into Saturday \nmorning. These winds will create choppy conditions on regional \nlakes and elevate fire weather concerns for ongoing wildfires and \nprescribed burns.\n\nBy this weekend, a cutoff low pressure system will track across \nthe region. Guidance continues to favor a northeasterly track, \nfocusing the highest probabilities for widespread wetting rain \nacross northwest Montana and Clearwater County, Idaho. Near the \ncenter of the low, snow levels lower to around 6,000 feet. \nAccumulations of 1 to 2 inches of snow are forecast for the higher\nterrain of Glacier National Park including Logan Pass, \nintroducing winter- like backcountry impacts. Seasonably cool and \nbreezy conditions will persist through Sunday.",
